== 4. False Inferences from Christ’s Work

#strong[The fourth #emph[device] that Satan has to keep souls from holy exercises, from religious services, is,]

By leading them to draw false inferences from the blessed and glorious things that Christ has done: that Jesus Christ has done all for us, and therefore there is nothing for us to do but to joy and rejoice. He has perfectly justified us, fulfilled the law, satisfied divine justice, pacified his Father’s wrath, and gone to heaven to prepare a place for us, and in the meantime to intercede for us; and therefore away with praying and mourning and hearing, etc. Ah, what a world of professing Christians Satan has drawn from religious services in these days, by leading them to draw such sad, wild and strange inferences from the sweet and excellent things that the Lord Jesus has done for his beloved ones!

#strong[Now the remedies against this device of Satan are as follows.]

The first #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is to dwell as much on the Scriptures that show you the duties and services Christ requires of you as on the Scriptures that declare#footnote[Tertullian has this expression of the fullness of the Scriptures (#emph[Adoro plenitudinem Scripturarum]): “I adore the fullness of the Scripture.” Gregory calls the Scripture (#emph[cor & animam Dei]) the heart and soul of God. Who then will not dwell on it?] to you the precious and glorious things that Christ has done for you. It is a sad and dangerous thing to have two eyes to behold our dignity and privileges, and not one eye to see our duties and services. I should look with one eye on the choice and excellent things that Christ has done for me, to raise my heart to love Christ with the purest love, to joy in Christ with the strongest joy, and to lift up Christ above all, who has made himself my all. And I should look with the other eye on the services and duties that the Scriptures require of those for whom Christ has done such blessed things, as in that word of the apostle: “Do you#super[+] not know that your body#footnote[1 Cor 6:19, 20.] is a temple of the Holy Spirit who is in you, whom you have received from God? You#super[+] are not your own; you were bought at a price. Therefore glorify God with your#super[+] body, and in your spirit, which are God’s.” And that one#footnote[1 Cor 15:58.]: “Therefore, my beloved brothers, be steadfast and immovable. Always excel in the work of the Lord, because you#super[+] know that your labor in the Lord is not in vain.” And that one: “Let us not grow weary in well-doing, for in due#footnote[Gal 6:9.] time we will reap a harvest if we do not give up.” And that of the apostle: “Rejoice at all times; pray without ceasing.” And that in Philippians: “Work out your#super[+] salvation with#footnote[Phil 2:12, 13. 1 Cor 11:26. Heb 10:24, 25.] fear and trembling.” And that one: “Do this until I come.” And that one: “Let us consider how to spur one another on to love and good deeds. Let us not neglect meeting together, as some have made a habit,#footnote[The Jews were much given to turning over the leaves of the Scripture, but they did not weigh their matter, John 5:39. “You search the Scriptures”: in the Greek it seems to be indicative rather than imperative.] but let us encourage one another, and all the more as you#super[+] see the Day approaching.” Now a soul that would not be drawn away by this device of Satan must not look with a squint eye on these blessed Scriptures, and many more of the same kind. He must dwell on them; he must make these Scriptures his chief and choicest companions. This will be a happy means to keep him close to Christ and his service in these times, when many turn their backs on Christ on the pretense of having a great share in the great and glorious things that Christ has done, etc.

The second #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is to consider that the great and glorious things Jesus Christ has done, and is doing, for us should be so far from taking us away from religious services and godly performances that they should be the greatest possible motives and encouragements to perform them, as the Scriptures abundantly show. I#footnote[1 Pet 2:9. Luke 1:74, 75. This I am sure of: all man’s happiness here is his holiness, and his holiness will hereafter be his happiness. Christ has broken the devil’s yoke off our necks for this very reason, that his Father might have better service from our hearts. Compare 2 Cor 6:16, 17, 18 with 7:1.] will give only some instances, such as this: “that we, being delivered from the hands of our enemies, may serve Him without fear, in holiness and righteousness before Him all the days of our lives.” Christ has freed you from all your enemies: from the curse of the law, the reigning, damning power of sin, the wrath of God, the sting of death and the torments of hell. But what is Christ’s end and design in doing these great and marvelous things for his people? It is not that they should throw off duties of righteousness and holiness, but that their hearts may be all the more free and sweet in all holy duties and heavenly services. So the apostle: “I will be their God, and they will be My people. I will be a Father to you#super[+], and you will be My sons and daughters, says the Lord Almighty.” (Mark what follows.) “Therefore, beloved, since we have these promises, let us cleanse ourselves from everything that defiles body and spirit, perfecting holiness in the fear of God.” And again: “The grace of God has appeared,#footnote[Titus 2:11, 12, 13, 14.] bringing salvation to everyone. It instructs us to renounce ungodliness and worldly passions, and to live sensible, upright, and godly lives in the present age, as we await the blessed hope and glorious#footnote[#emph[Tace lingua loquere vita]: do not talk of a good life, but let your life speak. Your actions, in passing, do not pass away; for every good work is a grain of seed for eternal life.] appearance of our great God and Savior Jesus Christ. He gave Himself for us to redeem us from all lawlessness and to purify for Himself a people for His own possession, zealous for good deeds.” Ah souls! I know no arguments that so work you#super[+] to a lively and constant performance of all heavenly services as those drawn from considering the great and glorious things that Christ has done for you. If such arguments will not take hold of you#super[+] and win you over, I do think that throwing hellfire in your faces will never do it.

The third #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is solemnly to consider that those precious souls for whom Jesus Christ#footnote[The saints’ #emph[motto] in all ages has been (#emph[Laboremus]) “Let us be doing.”] has done and suffered as much as he has for you have been exceedingly active and lively in all religious services and heavenly performances. He did as much and suffered as much for David#footnote[“God loves (#emph[curristas,] not #emph[quaeristas]) the runner, not the questioner or disputer,” says Luther.] as for you, and yet who was more in praying and praising God than David? “Seven times a day I praise You.” Who was more in studying and meditating on the word than David? “Your law is my meditation day and night.” You may read the same truth as you run in Jacob, Moses, Job, Daniel, and in the rest of the holy prophets and apostles, for whom Christ has done as much as for you. Ah, how all those worthies have abounded in works of righteousness and holiness, to the praise of free grace! Certainly Satan has got the upper hand of those souls who argue like this:#footnote[The day is at hand when God will require of men #emph[non quid legerint, sed quid egerint, nec quid dixerint, sed quomodo vixerint] \[not what they have read, but what they have done; not what they have said, but how they have lived\]. He who talks of heaven but does not do the will of God is like the man who gazed at the moon but fell into the pit.] “Christ has done such and such glorious things for us; therefore we need not make it any matter of care or conscience to do such and such religious services, as men say the word calls for.” If this logic is not from hell, what is? Ah, if the holy prophets and apostles were alive to hear such logic come out of the mouths of those who profess to have a share in the great and glorious things that Jesus Christ has done for his chosen ones, how they would blush to look on such souls! And how their hearts would grieve and break within them to hear the language and observe the actions of such souls!

The fourth #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is seriously to consider this: those who do not walk in ways of righteousness and holiness (who do not wait on God in the various duties and services that he commands) cannot have such evidence in their own souls of their righteousness before God, of their fellowship and communion#footnote[Certainly it is one thing to judge by our graces, another thing to rest or put our trust in them. There is a great deal of difference between declaring and deserving.] with God, of their blessedness here and their happiness hereafter, as those souls have who love and delight in the ways of the Lord, who are always at their best when they are most in the works and service of the Lord. “Little children,” says the apostle, “let no one deceive you#super[+]: The one who practices righteousness is righteous, just as Christ is righteous.” “By this,” says the same apostle, “the children of God are distinguished from the children of the devil: Anyone who does not practice righteousness is not of God, nor is anyone#footnote[As David’s daughters were known by their garments of many colors, so are God’s children known by their piety and holiness.] who does not love his brother.” “If you#super[+] know that He is righteous,” says the same apostle, “you also know that everyone who practices righteousness has been born of Him. If anyone says, ‘I know Him,’ but does not keep His commandments, he is a liar, and the truth is not in him. But if anyone keeps His word, the love of God has been truly perfected in him. By this we know that we are in Him: Whoever claims to abide in Him must walk as Jesus#footnote[A Christian’s emblem should be a house walking toward heaven. High words surely make a man neither holy nor just, but a virtuous life, a circumspect walk, makes him dear to God. A tree that is not fruitful is for the fire. Christianity is not a talking but a walking with God, who will not be put off with words. If he misses fruit, he will take up his axe, and then the soul is cut off forever.] walked. If we say we have fellowship with Him yet walk in the darkness, we lie and do not practice the truth. But if we walk in the light as He is in the light, we have fellowship with one another, and the blood of Jesus His Son cleanses us from all sin,” says the same apostle. So James in his second chapter: “What good is it, my brothers, if someone claims to have faith, but has no works? Can such faith save him?” That is, it cannot; for as the body without the spirit is dead, so faith without works is dead. To look after holy and heavenly works is the best way to keep the soul from being deceived and deluded by Satan’s delusions and by sudden flashes of joy and comfort; for holy works are a more tangible and constant pledge of the precious Spirit, begetting and maintaining in the soul a more solid, pure, clear, strong and lasting joy. Ah souls! If you#super[+] would have in yourselves a constant and blessed evidence of your fellowship with the Father and the Son, and of the truth of grace, and of your future happiness, see that you cleave close to holy services, and that you do not turn your backs on religious duties.

The fifth #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is solemnly to consider that#footnote[It is a precious truth never to be forgotten (#emph[Quod non actibus, sed finibus pensantur officia]) that duties are valued not by their acts but by their ends.] there are other choice and glorious ends for the saints’ performance of religious duties than the justifying of their persons before God, or the satisfying of the law or justice of God, or the purchasing of the pardon of sin, etc. Namely: to testify to their justification (“a good tree cannot help bearing good fruit”); to testify to their love to God and their sincere obedience to the commands of God; to testify to their deliverance from spiritual bondage; to give evidence of the indwelling#footnote[#emph[Finis movet ad agendum]: the end moves to action.] of the Spirit; to stop the mouths of the worst of men; and to gladden those righteous souls whom God would not have saddened. These, and many other choice ends, are why those who have a share in the glorious works of Christ should, in spite of that, keep close to the holy duties and religious services commanded#footnote[“#emph[Tene mensuram & respice finem]: keep yourself within bounds, and always have an eye to the end of your life and actions,” was the Emperor Maximilian’s #emph[motto].] by Christ. And if these considerations will not prevail with you#super[+] to wait on God in holy and heavenly duties, I am afraid that if one rose from the dead, his arguments would not win you over, but you would hold on in your sins and neglect his service, though you lost your souls forever, etc.
